WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.762
One common failure is trusting client-side validation.

2
00:00:03.762 --> 00:00:11.207
Browser checks and frontend form rules improve usability, but clients can be bypassed, modified, or out of date.

3
00:00:11.207 --> 00:00:14.028
The server must validate as the authority.

4
00:00:14.028 --> 00:00:18.496
Another common failure is binding directly to persistence models.

5
00:00:18.496 --> 00:00:32.759
When an Entity Framework entity or database-facing model is exposed as an input model, clients may be able to set fields such as role, owner ID, tenant ID, status, or audit values that should be controlled by the server.

6
00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:42.242
Overposting, also called mass assignment, happens when user-supplied fields are copied into properties that should not be user-controlled.

7
00:00:42.242 --> 00:00:48.277
DTOs reduce this risk by defining exactly what the client may send for a specific operation.

8
00:00:48.277 --> 00:00:53.292
Mapping should be intentional rather than automatic for sensitive fields.

9
00:00:53.292 --> 00:01:05.361
Reviewers should pay close attention to object spread equivalents, generic update helpers, patch endpoints, and any code that copies request properties into persistence objects.

10
00:01:05.361 --> 00:01:08.496
Validation is one layer, not the whole defense.

11
00:01:08.496 --> 00:01:18.684
SQL injection should be addressed primarily with parameterized queries or safe ORM patterns, not by hoping validation rejected every dangerous string.

12
00:01:18.684 --> 00:01:28.402
Command injection risk should be addressed by avoiding shell execution when possible and by using safe process APIs and strict argument handling.

13
00:01:28.402 --> 00:01:36.867
Path traversal should be addressed with safe path resolution and directory containment checks, not filename filtering alone.

14
00:01:36.867 --> 00:01:42.117
Business logic validation gaps can be more damaging than simple format errors.

15
00:01:42.117 --> 00:01:51.208
A request can be well formed but still violate ownership, payment limits, workflow state, approval requirements, or tenant boundaries.

16
00:01:51.208 --> 00:01:58.889
Strong validation programs connect input checks, authorization checks, domain invariants, safe APIs, and tests.

17
00:01:58.889 --> 00:02:01.710
The result is not a single giant validator.

18
00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:06.883
It is a layered design where each part enforces the rules it is responsible for.
